WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:04.430
- Are those caffeine pills,
at first, they're so exciting.

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:06.669
And then it gets even more
exciting, but after that,

00:00:06.669 --> 00:00:08.950
it gets so scary and in the end you ruin

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.250
your girl group shot at
a recording contract.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.083
- What?

00:00:12.083 --> 00:00:13.610
- Watching "Saved By The Bell" growing up,

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.610
I always thought it was
interesting that they never really

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.950
talk about Mario Lopez
or Slater being Mexican.

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:20.670
So as an Latino, watching this,

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.010
it was so refreshing to see the both

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.820
of you sort of take the
lead in "Saved By The Bell."

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:29.521
It was so, so cool, but
obviously, you guys play Daisy

00:00:29.521 --> 00:00:33.120
and Aisha, low-income students, transfer

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.930
to Bayside high school,
school full of rich kids.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.320
How did the both of you balance,

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.123
this really serious topic,
multiple series topics

00:00:41.123 --> 00:00:44.163
with humor as well, Alicia,
why don't you start?

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.280
- It's an honor to be on this show

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.960
that has such notoriety all over the world

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.540
and has had such a special
place in people's hearts,

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.250
and then have us two Latinos spearhead it.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.200
And I think one of the best
parts of our show is that we're

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.910
both from the same culture,

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:04.190
but our own distinct human
beings where these Latino women

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.760
with our own separate interests
and goals and passions.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.830
And I think it really
touches upon the fact

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:11.190
that the Latinx community
is not a monolith,

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.920
and we have our own views and
we hope that we empower people

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.820
within our own community,
but as actors and performers,

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.990
it's been so fun, just
exploring the juxtaposition

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.520
between having these very
serious conversations that need

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.530
to be had, and really reflect
the world we live in today.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.530
In addition to these
moments that are wacky

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:32.920
and unconventional, I think
it's one of the best parts

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.730
of our show, I think it disarms people

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:37.130
and hopefully shows people that no matter

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.230
where you come from or
where you look like,

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:41.770
these are things that you can
care about and conversations

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.180
about equity and representation
and diversity are things

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.530
that you should be
discussing in your household.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:47.363
So it's awesome.

00:01:48.515 --> 00:01:49.348
- Yes.

00:01:49.348 --> 00:01:50.673
(all laughing)

00:01:50.673 --> 00:01:53.090
(indistinct)

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:54.873
- Yeah girl

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:57.510
- I second that noise.

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:01.840
I seriously second that, I mean,
I grew up, not seeing a lot

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.860
of representation on TV
and I was always told

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.400
that I can do anything and be anything.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.160
But I think as a young Latina,
it's always helpful in a way

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.700
to see someone that looks like
you, that speaks like you,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.500
that has the same
background as you, because,

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.650
there's times where you may
get down on yourself because

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.430
that's a real thing, but to be able

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:23.610
to see someone being
represented in a certain light,

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.650
in a certain way, in a
certain form is so crucial

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.610
to someone's upbringing and mindset

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.870
and the way they take
information about themselves

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:34.365
and how they feel about themselves.

00:02:34.365 --> 00:02:36.210
So I love the fact that you
not only have one Latino,

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.950
but you have two Latinas, and
two very different Latinas,

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.470
but also very similar in ways.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:45.130
And, and I get to rock some, some curls

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:47.910
and you see, yeah, it's good.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.888
They did a good job, I'm proud
of you, I'm proud of them.

00:02:50.888 --> 00:02:52.490
- No, they really did.

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:55.328
Honestly, it was so dope
watching the both of you.

00:02:55.328 --> 00:02:56.920
And I'm not just going to reiterate

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.910
everything you guys just said.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.240
Lisa, you mentioned the
wacky and the unconventional.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:02.947
That's what we loved about
Saved By The Bell a lot,

00:03:02.947 --> 00:03:04.765
and this is stuff.

00:03:04.765 --> 00:03:06.360
I remember watching a show
Saturday morning, eating cereal.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.030
I'm like, this doesn't make any sense.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:10.770
What was it like for the both of you?

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.320
Because we, what's cool is
that in this re-imagining

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:14.600
of the show, we, we still get

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.080
those wacky informational moments.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.210
So what was it like for
the both of you to sort

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:21.140
of be the only rational
people at Bayside high school,

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:23.410
is Haskiri wanting you to take this one?

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:28.410
- I feel like I'm always rational
almost in my outside life

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:31.406
as, or hopefully I hope I am rational,

00:03:31.406 --> 00:03:34.520
but being able to see that rationality,

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:38.950
I think plays very well
because then you really see

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:42.210
the difference between the
Douglas kids and the Bayside kids

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:45.520
and what separates them,
and how different they are

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.240
in their thought process.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.210
And you kind of get to see that play off

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:52.840
on how each character kind
of rubs off on each other.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.920
I get a lot of Lexi and a lot of Max kind

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:58.700
of way of doing things.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.960
It's like not being able to
take charge and not being able

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:05.890
to enter a room and feel like
I'm taking up too much space

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.210
because that's how, what they do.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:10.840
And I'm like, you know what,
I'm gonna do that same thing.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.610
And then on the other side, I
feel like for their characters

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:15.930
is like, oh wow, Daisy is passionate

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.820
and she sees things differently
and she has a voice,

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:21.040
not only for herself, but
for everyone surrounding her.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:24.780
Maybe I need to take a step
back and see that for myself

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.630
and kind of take that on as a
trait in, as a characteristic.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:30.827
- We got to see that too,

00:04:30.827 --> 00:04:32.390
because your character
breaks a fourth wall,

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:34.570
which I think is really
awesome that they can see

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:37.120
through Daisy's eyes
"Saved By The Bell" is one

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.560
of the best things, shows
to come out of the nineties.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.020
I love the nineties and rocking Tommy,

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:45.060
what is your favorite nineties trend?

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.890
- I always say high
waisted jeans, high waist,

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:52.500
crops, crop tops, I
own a lot of crop tops.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.060
I don't think I own a regular size t-shirt

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:56.290
(laughing)

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:57.230
I need to go shopping.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.170
- That's actually a fact.

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:02.210
- Yeah, right everyday on set
that I did not have a crop.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.780
- Anything like, but you look
phenomenal on your crop tops.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:06.327
So keep wearing your crop top.

00:05:06.327 --> 00:05:10.750
Specifically for me, I am in
love with the nineties culture,

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:12.970
but I think something really
cool about our new show

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:17.430
is that I, as Aisha wear
a lot of nineties fashion,

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.910
more reminiscent of the
nineties hip hop culture,

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:22.490
which we didn't really
see in the original "Saved

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:26.003
By The Bell," so you'll see,
Aisha wearing like really big,

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.590
similar to like TLC
overalls and door knockers.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:33.350
So she definitely honors
the nineties in her fashion,

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:35.792
in this new cool, reinvented way.

00:05:35.792 --> 00:05:36.700
(rap music)

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:37.743
- Can we talk?

00:05:37.743 --> 00:05:39.370
- Did you just sit backwards in a chair?

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:40.940
Wow, I've never seen anyone
do that in real life.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.100
- I am so stoked to talk to you guys.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.480
My first question is
for you Belmont 'cause

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.720
you play Jesse's son, but you're sort

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.650
of like the Slater in this show.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:53.710
So I was curious, you
got a chance to sit down

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:56.390
with Maria Lopez or Elizabeth
Berkley and talk to them

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:59.790
about your character and what
you wanted to bring in Jamie.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:00.900
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.744
I remember, well, I met Elizabeth during

00:06:03.744 --> 00:06:06.560
the audition process and
then when we were shooting

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.520
the pilot and that first week.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.470
I had wrapped kind of
around the middle of the day

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:13.410
and Elizabeth was on
set just as a producer.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:15.290
And we were sitting in video village

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:18.160
where there was everybody
watching the monitors,

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.410
and then it was somehow hours later.

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:22.450
We were just two people sitting in chairs

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:25.410
in this empty space, having
this wonderful conversation,

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:29.017
talking about how the
dynamic is, and the family,

00:06:29.017 --> 00:06:31.000
and the relationship
between the two of them.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:36.000
And yes, Jamie and Mario have
some similarities as well.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:39.660
So I got, kinda got to dive
into those similarities with him

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.510
and just meeting both of
those original cast members

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:45.490
and working with them has
been an absolute blessing.

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:47.450
It is a very cool experience.

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:48.930
- What from Mario Slater,

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.900
did you wanna bring in
a Jamie specifically?

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:56.900
- Well, there's like this
competitive jockey sports aspect

00:06:57.070 --> 00:06:59.540
to Slater that Jamie totally has.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:02.750
The only difference is
Slater is genuinely athletic

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:04.578
and Jamie is clueless.

00:07:04.578 --> 00:07:06.828
(laughing)

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:13.215
- And Dexter, you actually
got to shoot with Mario solo.

00:07:13.215 --> 00:07:15.650
I got to see it a couple
of episodes, so tell me

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:18.220
what that was like, and do
you have any fun stories

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.715
from set shooting with him
specifically that you can share.

00:07:20.715 --> 00:07:24.307
- Yeah of course, I
mean, when I got the role

00:07:24.307 --> 00:07:26.710
and I found out that the
majority of my scenes were going

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:30.610
to be with him in the pilot,
it definitely just brought me

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:32.880
in a little ounce of
nervousness, if I'm being

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.560
a hundred percent transparent with you,

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:35.820
but excitement as well.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:38.080
And then of course, meeting
Mario and chatting with him

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.150
and he gives you that
smile and then brings you

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:42.050
in for that handshake and hug.

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:44.930
It makes everything go much
more easy and it allows you

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:46.970
the opportunity and the space
to be able to create something

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:48.920
really, really beautiful and special.

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:51.776
- Mario and I have so many
mutual interests, from dance,

00:07:51.776 --> 00:07:54.500
from being pop culture
nerds of the eighties

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:55.333
and the nineties.

00:07:55.333 --> 00:07:58.485
And so, we were always
dancing around on the set

00:07:58.485 --> 00:08:01.050
in trailers and shooting Instagram videos.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.500
And his daughter is very big on TickTock.

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:05.660
And so whenever, she was
around, we would be making

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:07.790
them some TickTok dances
and stuff like that.

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:11.410
So Mario and Elizabeth, both,
actually all, every single OG

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:14.967
that we brought back really
made it a point to just sow

00:08:14.967 --> 00:08:17.479
into us and allowed us know that,

00:08:17.479 --> 00:08:20.570
hey, we know we were the
first, but you guys are the now

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:22.700
and we appreciate you and we support you.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:25.830
And so having that really
was, was really fantastic.

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:27.240
- That's a really nice sentiment,

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:29.640
especially because your character Devante,

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:32.610
I hope I'm saying it right,
because it was funny how

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:34.240
you pronounced my name in the show.

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:35.700
Whoever's watching this
and you watched the show

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:39.170
that'll make sense, but I
wanted to ask the both of you,

00:08:39.170 --> 00:08:41.730
is there any, did you go back and watch,

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:45.240
the original and it's kind
of fun to poke fun at sort

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:48.255
of the corniness and that
she missed from that show.

00:08:48.255 --> 00:08:51.970
So what was easy for
you guys to naturally,

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:55.470
poke fun at this time
around from the original.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:58.330
- It is fun Tracy Wakefield,

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:01.490
our show runner did such
a phenomenal job of toeing

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:05.000
that line between being
self-aware about the corniness

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.325
of the original show,
and then still honoring

00:09:07.325 --> 00:09:11.320
the beautiful aspects of the
original that everybody loves.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:13.640
Really just walking that tightrope,

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:14.980
Tracy did a phenomenal job.

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:18.820
And so it was very fun, but
humor is a little different,

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:21.667
just like the show is
different because it's newer,

00:09:21.667 --> 00:09:24.640
but I'm excited about all the jokes.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:27.723
Yeah, growing up, such a big
fan of the original show,

00:09:28.744 --> 00:09:33.580
like I love Zach, Kelly,
Screech, Slater, Lisa, Jesse,

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:34.740
like I loved them growing up,

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.330
but I do also understand
that "Saved By The Bell"

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:40.660
in the nineties is not
work, does not work in 2020.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:44.160
So I think like, like
Belmont said so beautifully,

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:46.420
Tracy and our producer
Franco, who was a part

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:49.940
of the original series, found
a really, really great way

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:54.220
to combine the nuance
and the nostalgic aspect

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:57.141
of the original show with the
groundedness and the reality

00:09:57.141 --> 00:09:59.070
of the world that we live in today.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:02.035
And I hope that it
continues to be transformed,

00:10:02.035 --> 00:10:03.460
like translated that way
and understood that way.

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:06.360
- What is your favorite thing
to come out of the nineties?

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:08.573
Favorite nineties trends.

00:10:09.536 --> 00:10:12.500
- Oooh, the Jordan one, the Jordan one,

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:13.670
the hot top Jordan one.

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:17.190
It could be the OG, all the
different color already.

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:20.200
All iterations, I'm a sneaker
head, I love sneakers.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.851
And so then now my accent just
made like am a sneaker head.

00:10:23.851 --> 00:10:24.835
- Yeah the accent.

00:10:24.835 --> 00:10:25.668
(laughs)

00:10:25.668 --> 00:10:28.270
- I immediately get right back
to my South Jersey cities,

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:31.020
but yeah, the Jordan ones
is probably the best thing

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:33.550
to come out of the nineties for myself.

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:35.933
- For me, it's gotta be the
music, I love all, I still,

00:10:35.933 --> 00:10:38.781
most of the music I listened
to it comes from the nineties.

00:10:38.781 --> 00:10:41.693
REM, Weezer, the police,
all that good stuff,

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:45.515
but yeah, I'm a sneaker head too.

00:10:45.515 --> 00:10:46.844
(laughing)

00:10:46.844 --> 00:10:49.200
- And do you only know how
Bayside works for kids?

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:50.260
- Hot kids?

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:51.140
- Privileged kids.

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:52.020
- Privileged kids.

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:55.610
- Oh my God, Lexi, your
character is just everything.

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:59.360
Serving books, best one
liner, she's transgender.

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:01.930
We so rarely get that
type of representation

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:04.780
in the TV space, let alone
the high school TV space.

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:07.490
So first wanted to ask
you, what was it like

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:10.330
and how did you feel bringing,
being able to bring Lexi

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:12.250
to life with that in mind?

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:14.640
- I mean, she is literally
the most fun character

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:18.010
I've probably ever played,
getting to wear six inch heels

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:20.090
to algebra two in the morning.

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:23.760
It was an experience I never
thought I would live through.

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:26.749
She is so fun and there's
so much to her that I think

00:11:26.749 --> 00:11:30.160
is so interesting and
entertaining to watch throughout

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:34.610
her experience on the show
and her arc as a human being.

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:37.330
I think you see evolve
throughout the season.

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:40.260
And I think that's really
topical for so many people

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:43.360
in America right now, having
this kind of reckoning,

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:45.210
where they're realizing
their privileged bubble

00:11:45.210 --> 00:11:46.043
isn't everything.

00:11:46.043 --> 00:11:48.110
And I really love that about her

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:51.000
and the other Bayside
kids are in the show.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:54.390
- What did it mean to you to
be able to bring a transgender

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:57.080
character to life in sort of this space?

00:11:57.080 --> 00:11:59.430
Because we so rarely
get that representation.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:00.520
- Yeah it meant everything to me.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:04.090
I think when Tracy came
to me about wanting

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:08.836
to do the show, it
wasn't originally planned

00:12:08.836 --> 00:12:12.880
for her to be transgender, but
the more we talked about it,

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:16.680
the more we saw an opportunity
to have representation like

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:19.250
this onscreen, because so
often trans representation,

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:23.620
as we know, usually has to
do with the trans struggle

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:26.320
or puts trans people in a negative light,

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:27.440
in a negative experience.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:31.220
So getting to tell a story and
serve as a producer on a show

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:34.190
where I had input on making
sure it was authentic

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:36.990
as possible was the
greatest opportunity ever,

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:40.340
because I think with me
and Tracy coming together

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:43.920
and our writers, we really
told a story that isn't all

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:46.020
about her gender, and it's so much more

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:49.990
than just her gender, but
does tackle that part of her,

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:52.419
which I think is really, really important.

00:12:52.419 --> 00:12:54.380
- So incredible and so needed.

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:56.420
And can't wait to see so
many more, and you kill it

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:58.690
and saw your also, producer on the show.

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:00.647
So congratulations for that, it's amazing.

00:13:00.647 --> 00:13:01.480
- [Josie] Thank you

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:03.880
Obviously, you know,
Lexi's this, she's awesome.

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:06.560
She's this cheerleader, most popular girl,

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:07.883
popular girl in school.

00:13:08.753 --> 00:13:10.201
We've seen this archetype before,

00:13:10.201 --> 00:13:12.500
so who are your main influences for Lexi?

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:15.250
Like did you go back and watch
like Regina George clips?

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:17.470
Like who was your main influence?

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:21.990
- My biggest influence
for Lexi was the dreams

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:25.100
that I had growing up as a young child.

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:28.860
Only wishing I could dress, talk, act half

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:30.170
the way Lexi does.

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:35.170
So that was my full on sort
of, I would say inspiration

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:37.810
for the character, but the more I did it,

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:41.430
the more I found myself talking a lot

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:44.290
like Catherine O'Hara in "Schitt's Creek."

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:45.800
I've literally said
this in every interview,

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:47.610
I guess what I'm saying
here is I'm obsessed

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:49.320
with Catherine O'Hara
and "Schitt's Creek."

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:52.690
I wish I was her, and
I'm sad that I'm not.

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:57.690
But I just found myself like,
speaking in weird dialects

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:02.630
in the same vein that Catherine
does in that wonderful show.

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:07.630
So yeah, I'd say a mixture
of her and of just my dreams.

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:11.530
- That's, honestly iconic
Moira Rose is literally

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:12.993
my favorite character and
I'm happy you said that

00:14:12.993 --> 00:14:16.909
because I wanted to ask where
you got Lexi's mannerisms

00:14:16.909 --> 00:14:19.030
and just the way she moves her hands.

00:14:19.030 --> 00:14:21.380
I wasn't sure if it was
just how you are or,

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:23.940
but yeah, watching Schitt's
Creek, like I notice you sort

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:27.090
of whisper your last line,
when you're saying things

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:29.360
like you'll sort of lower your voice.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:31.410
So I wanted to know
where all that came from.

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:33.100
Did that come from why you Maira Rose?

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:35.240
- Yeah, I mean, there were
certain things that came

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:37.380
from her, but honestly just
me and my regular life.

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:40.510
People that know me know that
I'm an insane human being.

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:43.020
Like I have moments where
I can be a humble child

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:46.420
that speaks normally, but I
also have moments where I am in,

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:50.100
person from Mars and I'm
not making any sense.

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:52.680
But what I thought was
actually funny is throughout

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:55.380
the show, I like wanted to
find new ways of holding

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:57.120
like my little handbags.

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:00.120
And I saw that the girl who played Alexis

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:03.274
on "Schitt's Creek" like
had the same sort of idea

00:15:03.274 --> 00:15:04.950
of like finding new ways to do it.

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:07.730
So she did like the pretend handbag thing.

00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:11.900
And my thing was holding a
handbag with just one finger.

00:15:11.900 --> 00:15:16.900
And you'll see, I believe it's
in episode seven where I have

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:19.900
this tiny purse that
is literally big enough

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:21.592
for an edamame AP.

00:15:21.592 --> 00:15:25.240
And like, that's it maybe
like half of a lip gloss tool.

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:27.960
And throughout the
entire episode, I hold it

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:30.550
with a single finger, so
that'll be fun for people.

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:33.670
- Say, that is so amazing,
now it's like my dream answer,

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:36.320
because I've done a lot of
research on how Alexis took

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:37.533
this and made it this.

00:15:37.533 --> 00:15:39.290
So I just want to ask
you one last question.

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:42.600
What is the best thing to come
out of the nineties for you?

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:46.540
- The best thing to come
out of the nineties for me

00:15:47.667 --> 00:15:52.667
is probably the fashion,
the low rise Jean moment,

00:15:52.829 --> 00:15:57.829
the messy, dirty girl hair
moment and the spice lip.

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:01.080
I think we need to bring it back.

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:04.250
She deserves to be researched,
so those things, I would say.

00:16:04.250 --> 00:16:05.770
- My dad's also coming.

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:08.000
- I don't care about your dad's Spencer.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:09.980
I just care about Zach and Kelly's

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:12.590
- hello, John, should I
call you John or Michael?

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:14.880
I don't, I feel like you
go by Michael, right?

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:17.520
- I do go by Michael, most
people don't know that,

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:19.690
but yeah, if you wanna get my, if I safe

00:16:19.690 --> 00:16:21.590
is about to fall on my head, say Michael,

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:23.024
because I'll move on.

00:16:23.024 --> 00:16:24.700
If you call me John I have
to think for a minute,

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:26.090
I was like, Oh, I'm John.

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:27.286
So.

00:16:27.286 --> 00:16:28.119
(laughs)

00:16:28.119 --> 00:16:30.204
- Michael, there's a
thief coming, amazing.

00:16:30.204 --> 00:16:31.037
- Aaargh!

00:16:31.037 --> 00:16:31.870
(laughs)

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:34.170
I have waited a very long
time to interview you.

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:36.690
Your career is just so varied.

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:38.520
You've played so many
different characters.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:41.910
What was it like coming into
the role of principal Todman

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:44.243
and playing a high school principal?

00:16:44.243 --> 00:16:47.341
- It's not the first high school
principal I've ever played.

00:16:47.341 --> 00:16:49.352
I think I've played about 94 of them.

00:16:49.352 --> 00:16:50.451
(laughs)

00:16:50.451 --> 00:16:54.530
And it's one of the things I
do a lot, anything that works

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:56.240
with a tie, that's what I do.

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:57.073
(Naz laughs)

00:16:57.073 --> 00:17:00.840
But I actually love this show.

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:03.360
I've really, I've become
very attached to this show.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:08.360
And I think part of it is that
my colleagues are so young

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:14.460
and talented, and I sort of
am reminded of when I was

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:18.380
a young actor and how
I engaged with the work

00:17:18.380 --> 00:17:21.930
and how I was changed by
it and things like that.

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:25.210
And I've been doing it so
long now that you do forget

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:27.840
these things and they're very important,

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:32.840
And I have just been sort
of enlivened by this job.

00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:37.330
Strange, I really have,
cause I'm one of the only,

00:17:37.330 --> 00:17:40.057
like there's just a
few adults in the cast,

00:17:40.057 --> 00:17:42.970
and it's really thrilling to work

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:46.560
with that much collegians frankly.

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:48.371
- Right, collegians.

00:17:48.371 --> 00:17:50.240
(both laughing)

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:51.280
Michael you are hilarious.

00:17:51.280 --> 00:17:53.390
Yeah, no, to being around someone,

00:17:53.390 --> 00:17:56.040
that seeing something for the
first time is very exciting.

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:56.873
- It is.

00:17:56.873 --> 00:17:58.670
- And to hear you say
that it livened it up,

00:17:58.670 --> 00:18:01.060
it sort of warms my heart
to hear you say that,

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:02.573
that's really cool.

00:18:02.573 --> 00:18:04.220
You've played so many principles
that obviously this is a

00:18:04.220 --> 00:18:07.470
reboot, so did you
channel Mr. Belding at all

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:10.880
or reference his performance for Todman?

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:13.968
- Well, yeah, we like to say that the show

00:18:13.968 --> 00:18:16.010
is a re-imagining, not so much a reboot

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:18.320
because there are so many new characters.

00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:23.240
Really, Mario's character and
Elizabeth Berkley's character

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:26.370
are the only characters
that are in the regular cast

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:29.877
that are returning from the
old show, from the nineties.

00:18:29.877 --> 00:18:34.300
I'm a totally different person
than Dennis Haskins played.

00:18:34.300 --> 00:18:37.450
I have a different name, I have
a different set of problems,

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:39.347
I have a different personal life.

00:18:39.347 --> 00:18:41.780
So I don't really do, I didn't
need to do research there.

00:18:41.780 --> 00:18:43.580
What was useful for me
is to watch a couple

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:47.450
of the old episodes and watch
out Dennis Haskins negotiates

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:51.517
his performance amongst the
kids, because it's tricky.

00:18:51.517 --> 00:18:54.130
The sweet spot of the show
was watching these kids,

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:58.160
the group and friends, and
see what issues they have.

00:18:58.160 --> 00:19:01.120
And then this sort of referee,
this adult referee is walking

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:04.440
around mostly to be pranked
and to being demeaned

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:07.333
in some way, 'cause he's
the heavy, but also to sort

00:19:07.333 --> 00:19:09.800
of facilitate what they're doing and sort

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:11.330
of pull comedy out of them.

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:15.550
So that was very useful
to watch Dennis do that.

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:17.240
And he's super good.

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:19.200
- At the same time
there's also a lot of you

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:22.510
in this character, I know you've
come from Broadway Michael

00:19:22.510 --> 00:19:24.640
you've written vocal
arrangements for things

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:26.340
you've been in, I don't
wanna give too much away,

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:29.890
but obviously principal Todman
does have like a musical tie

00:19:29.890 --> 00:19:32.360
in the show, people will
see once they watch it.

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:33.587
So how did that come about?

00:19:33.587 --> 00:19:34.883
Was that your idea?

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:39.680
- Well, I think, Tracy Wakefield,

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:42.343
who's our creator on
this show and our writer,

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:45.120
she and I have a relationship.

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:47.340
She and I did a show together
called great "Great News"

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:48.990
on NBC a couple of years ago.

00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:53.990
And, so she, once she
gets to know a person,

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:58.733
me in particular, in this
case, she can write toward me,

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:02.710
and so a lot, she knows that
I'm musical and she knows

00:20:02.710 --> 00:20:06.390
that I do X well or Y well,
and she will go ahead and write

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:09.150
it in, because she likes to see me do it.

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:14.150
And she also unfortunately
knows what hurts for me.

00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:19.220
So she will make, she will demean me.

00:20:19.220 --> 00:20:22.480
I'm very often in the writing,
me personally, Michael,

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:27.480
so that, she knows that I
will be embarrassed by some

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:29.467
of the things that I have to say.

00:20:29.467 --> 00:20:30.300
(laughs)

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:31.600
She knows how to get me.

00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:34.690
So it's really, it's been a pleasure.

00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:36.190
- I am shocked to even hear you say

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:38.450
that you've been embarrassed
because just some of the stuff

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:41.140
that you've done is so,
I after one Martini,

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:44.152
I turn into Richard from the breakup.

00:20:44.152 --> 00:20:48.130
Oooh, how many times have you
been asked to do in real life?

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:51.313
- Oh my gosh, boy, you hit
it on the nose, I tell yah.

00:20:52.430 --> 00:20:57.361
Naz, I can't go into a grocery
store without somebody,

00:20:57.361 --> 00:21:01.730
pointing across the, the
coffee aisle and saying like,

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:05.630
there's Gary on the "Kick drum"
there, you know what I mean?

00:21:05.630 --> 00:21:09.220
It's happens all the time,
it's every day, almost, yeah.

00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:12.030
Just that one scene, you never know,

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:14.877
you never know you do a scene,
I didn't even remember it.

00:21:14.877 --> 00:21:19.110
And years later it's like, I
have to deal with it every day.

00:21:19.110 --> 00:21:20.696
It's crazy.

00:21:20.696 --> 00:21:23.279
(upbeat music)

